    "learn": [
      {
        "title": "Charge in motion",
        "text": "Electric current is the rate of flow of electric charge. A source of potential difference is needed to sustain current in a closed conducting circuit. In a metal the mobile charges are electrons; conventional current is drawn in the opposite direction, from positive to negative through the external circuit. Our moving dots show conventional direction, not electron drift speed.",
        "scene": "el1:current:0",
        "process": false
      },
      {
        "title": "Measure what passes a point",
        "text": "Charge flow Q, current I and time t obey Q=It. Use coulombs C, amperes A and seconds s. A current of 1 A means 1 C passes a cross-section each second. For 0.5 A over 8 s, Q=4 C. The animation counts cumulative charge past a reference line; it does not count charge being created inside the cell.",
        "scene": "el1:current:1",
        "process": true,
        "processFilm": "el1-current"
      },
      {
        "title": "Current is not used up",
        "text": "The current is the same at every point in a single closed loop. A lamp transfers energy to its surroundings, but charge continues around the circuit; less current does not emerge after the lamp. A steady state has no accumulating charge at a point. Branching circuits are a later lesson.",
        "scene": "el1:current:2",
        "process": false
      },
      {
        "title": "Rearrange and convert",
        "text": "Use I=Q/t or t=Q/I. For 12 C in 4 s, I=3 A. Convert 250 mA to 0.25 A and minutes to seconds before substituting. Compare currents 0.5/1 A and times 4/8 s in the model. Model current is an ideal supplied value; tracer spacing/speed is symbolic, not measured carrier motion.",
        "scene": "el1:current:3",
        "process": false
      }
    ],
