    "higher": false,
    "minutes": 40,
    "learn": [
      {
        "title": "Length is the investigated factor",
        "text": "For a wire of the same material and thickness at constant temperature, a longer length has greater resistance. Measure the selected length between contacts; measure the potential difference across that same segment and the current through it. Calculate resistance from R=V/I rather than assuming current alone measures resistance.",
        "scene": "el1:wire:0",
        "process": false
      },
      {
        "title": "Connect geometry with readings",
        "text": "Our original virtual wire compares lengths 0.25/0.50 m and two cross-section indices. Supplied potential difference is 1 V. The toy resistance rule is R=2L/A using an arbitrary material coefficient; this rule is not a required equation or a real wire rating. Read the virtual V and I, then predict R. Compare length at fixed thickness, and thickness at fixed length.",
        "scene": "el1:wire:1",
        "process": true,
        "processFilm": "el1-wire"
      },
      {
        "title": "Keep the test fair",
        "text": "Keep wire material, cross-section and temperature controlled when testing length. Keep the current low, switch off between readings and allow cooling so warming does not confound the results. Use suitable equipment and school risk assessment. Repeat readings, record length/V/I/R with units and plot resistance against length; identify anomalies rather than silently discarding them.",
        "scene": "el1:wire:2",
        "process": false
      },
      {
        "title": "Practical preparation has boundaries",
        "text": "This lesson prepares the wire-length part of Physics RP 3 / Trilogy RP 15, AT 1/6/7. The required practical also includes series and parallel resistor combinations, now available in the Resistor combinations lesson. A virtual table and graph do not establish physical setup, unrestricted graph drawing, measuring uncertainty or supervised practical competence.",
        "scene": "el1:wire:3",
        "process": false
      }
    ],
    "big": "For a wire of the same material and thickness at constant temperature, a longer length has greater resistance. Measure the selected length between contacts; measure the potential difference across that same segment and the current through it. Calculate resistance from R=V/I rather than assuming current alone measures resistance.",
